ame has arrived for `wait_for_agent_silence_ms`. `max_silence_ms` is the wait budget (amended 2026-10-27, lead decision on the batch A reading 1; the earlier text, "once no active frame has arrived for `max_silence_ms`", could never end a comfort-noise call): the call ends with `max_silence` once the simulator has waited `max_silence_ms` since its wait for the agent began without being able to take its turn, whether the agent stayed silent or kept sending active frames. So an agent reply that starts later than `max_silence_ms` after the wait began, or a pause-free reply that is still running at that point, ends the call with `max_silence` (an owner consequence for the O4 values). Both are required values of `tracer.sim_profile/1` with no default in code (the shipped example profile is labelled as an example, and its values are owner values, O4); `caller_speaks_first` is required too; `activity_min_peak` is the one optional value of the object. A scripted turn may carry `barge_in_after_ms`: the caller starts that line that many milliseconds after the onset of agent activity instead of waiting for silence. The simulator never sends caller audio while the brain is still producing the next line. An agent that streams noise at or above the threshold never looks silent (without the member, any noise does), so such a call ends on `max_silence` (or `max_duration`) with its timing values without a second edge (ADR 0027), named in the outcome, never a hang. Rejected: a Silero VAD inside Tracer (it runs a model; owner rule); an energy or RMS window over several frames (a second number and a window nobody measured); a default in code other than 1 (a number nobody measured). Trade-off: the owner must measure the noise floor of the agent and write a number; a number set below the noise leaves the call ending `max_silence`, and a number set above the agent's speech makes the agent look silent for ever; a file without the number behaves as before. Tests: `test_a_frame_is_active_iff